Organizations that cannot send security data to hosted services increasingly specialize open-weight language models for cyber threat intelligence (CTI): mapping vulnerabilities to weakness classes, extracting attack techniques from incident descriptions, recommending mitigations, and attributing intrusions. Building such a model is a software-engineering problem: its developers must know where each training example came from, which data defects the pipeline's checks catch, and where in a multi-stage training pipeline a capability was gained or lost. This article reports the design and evaluation of Glaukopis, a pipeline that generates instruction-tuning data by executing typed queries over a knowledge graph of twelve public CTI sources, so that every CTI training target resolves to the graph facts that produced it, and that fine-tunes Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct in four stages while retaining and evaluating each stage's checkpoint. The evaluation uses three CTI benchmarks and a general-reasoning diagnostic, compares against the base model, a security-specialized open model, six hosted frontier models, and single-stage runs, and reports item-level bootstrap intervals and response-level error taxonomies. The final checkpoint raises the three-benchmark mean from 57.7 to 65.0, but the gain is confined to the benchmark whose task schemas the data targets; two independently built benchmarks are unchanged and general reasoning falls by 11.6 points. Checkpoint-level analysis traces two regressions to one stage of short structured-answer drills. A single-stage run on the same base reaches a comparable aligned score, so no advantage is claimed for the staged schedule. Seven lessons for engineering traceable, regression-aware domain specialization conclude the article.
